    "q-116571": {
      "dom_id": "step-q116571",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The period of the function is $\\pi .$\n\n- There is no minimum or maximum value.\n\n- The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…$\n\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},….$ At each asymptote, the graph tends to $\\infty $ from the left and $-\\infty $ from the right.\n\nOf the given plots, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254348/assets/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
